<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Makanga</h1><p class="st-meta">1850 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A warlord state north of the Zambezi founded by the Goan-Portuguese Pereira family and its Chikunda slave armies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Makanga grew from the estates of the Pereira family, Goan-Portuguese settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley. In the mid-19th century, as the Portuguese prazo system collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goncalo-caetano-pereira">Gonçalo Caetano Pereira</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-caetano-pereira">Pedro Caetano Pereira</a> built an autonomous state north of the river near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a>, ruled from fortified stockades called aringas.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power rested on armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikunda">Chikunda</a>, enslaved and freed soldier-hunters armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, who raided neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsenga">Nsenga</a> lands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and captives. The rulers acted like African chiefs, taking African titles and wives, while nominally recognising Portugal and often supplying it with troops against rival states such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massangano">Massangano</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Makanga remained effectively independent until the Portuguese, reasserting control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley, conquered it in 1902 and incorporated it into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikunda">Chikunda</a> communities descended from its soldiers survive in the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1850 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.52%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1850 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedro Caetano Pereira</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chikunda, Chichewa, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pereira state of Makanga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=makanga" aria-label="Makanga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5705 2695 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Makanga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5861 3386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6025 2951h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6025 2951h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mozambique. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=makanga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

